<p>
The 14 <code>itemtypes/wearable/seraph/*.json</code> files (one per wearable slot: head, face,
neck, shoulder, upperbody, ...) are each fully variant-expanded. Clothing has no recipe/drop table
of its own in survival (worldgen placement inside a container, the creative inventory, or a
character class's starting gear are the only sources), so -- like paintings/tapestries -- it never
joins the main item index and gets its own dedicated catalog instead.
</p>
<p>
"Found in structures" is left empty for every clothing item: worn/stored items live inside a
schematic's opaque block-entity inventory data (a wardrobe, a chest, ...), which isn't decoded for
this content type the way tapestries' own block-entity attributes are.
</p>

<h3>Warmth, protection and repair</h3>
<p>
Each wearable's stats (<code>models/wearables.ts</code>) are read per concrete variant from the same
<code>itemtypes/wearable/**</code> files: <code>attributes.warmth</code> (merged with the glob-matched
<code>attributesByType</code>), <code>durability</code>, <code>attributes.protectionModifiers</code> (relative protection, flat damage
reduction, protection tier, per-tier losses, falling back to the file's <code>defaultProtLoss</code>),
<code>attributes.statModifiers</code> and <code>attributes.rainProtectionPerc</code>. Clothes with warmth are repaired by whatever carries
<code>attributes.clothingRepairStrength</code> (sewing kit 75&nbsp;%, cloth and linen sheets 50&nbsp;%, flax twine 10&nbsp;%), applied in the
character inventory; armor is repaired in the crafting grid by the "... repair" recipes, listed on its item page. Armor itself is
crafted, so its page is an <a href="../items/">item page</a>, not a clothing page.
</p>
